      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The aim of our study was to evaluate the role of multidetector-row computed tomography (MDCT) in patients referred for heart valve surgery. We studied the diagnostic performance of CT coronary angiography (CTCA) compared with conventional coronary angiography (CCA) before valve surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "During a 13-month period, 55 consecutive patients under evaluation for aortic (40/55) or mitral valve (15/55) disease before potential valve replacement underwent CTCA using a 64-detector-row scanner within 2 months of CCA for comparative purposes. All 17 major coronary artery segments were evaluated by one observer and compared with the reference standard. Patient-based, vessel-based and segment-based analyses of the data were performed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Prevalence of significant coronary artery disease, defined as having at least one stenosis >/=50% per patient, was 36%. On a patient-based analysis, sensitivity, specificity and positive and negative predictive values were 100%, 91%, 83% and 100%, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The diagnostic accuracy of 64-row CTCA for ruling out the presence of significant coronary stenoses in patients undergoing valve surgery is excellent and allows CTCA to be used as a gatekeeper for invasive CCA in these patients. MDCT is a necessary preoperative examination that provides useful information for identifying potential operative complications of surgical procedures."
        }
      ]},
